Absolute temperature, units and reference levels (@th-scales: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW", #source "../../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "Equilibrium-state-variables-and-local-description.lingua#r_7ZE016Z66H53QB69W5F3HVEN5X") { r_36PSQZ15KGX9RBTGGBV1G7Q06S
**Read:** [Introduction to Engineering Thermodynamics — Claire Yu Yan](../sources/Introduction-to-Engineering-Thermodynamics-Yan.lingua#r_6AX93SRMGE0XXJTQ1NW6X9QXTW), properties, units and temperature; [Practical realizations of SI units — BIPM consultative committees](../../../../inquiry/metrology/sources/SI-Realizations.lingua#r_240WHEHDVW9TR241AXP6T4STM0), the complete applicable kelvin realization and SI context.

Use kelvin in temperature ratios, entropy transfer Q/T, radiation laws and equations of state. A temperature difference of 1 K equals a difference of 1 °C, while a temperature of 0 °C is 273.15 K. An offset scale cannot be substituted into a ratio simply because its differences are convenient.

Distinguish heat rate in W, heat flux in W/m², conductivity in W/(m·K), a convective coefficient in W/(m²·K), thermal resistance in K/W and volumetric heat capacity in J/(m³·K). Their products must represent the stated physical quantity. For a 2 kW input over 30 minutes, the supplied energy is 3.6 MJ; the final temperature still requires a material, mass, losses and possible phase change.

Pressure is absolute in an equation of state unless explicitly transformed from a gauge reading. Thermodynamic temperature, a practical temperature scale and sensor indication are distinct. Relative enthalpy/entropy zero points are conventions that must remain consistent across state tables and software.

